A||||5||Jncomutt||Passenger side oil leak||||1||
Z||000000||Jncomutt||11-14-2001||01:35 AM||||Ok, my 84 duke is leaking oil.  I'm not sure where its coming from.  I notice that the underside passenger half of the oil pan is oily.  The cradle/frame on the passenger side that front to back is also oily.  I can tell that its not the drain screw, its not the oil filter either.  What is up there than can leak??||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroSoul||11-14-2001||06:30 AM||||  One thing could be the front seal around the crank in the timing cover.  My 84 thew an A/C belt once. A piece of the belt wrapped around the crank behind the big pulley and caused a massive oil leak.  I had a rooster tail of oil shooting up between the engine vent and the battery cover. Big Mess!||||reg||6
Z||000002||Oreif||11-14-2001||09:26 AM||||Another spot prone to leaking is the panel on the side of the block that covers the lifters. This panel is only siliconed on from the factory.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jncomutt||11-16-2001||11:17 PM||||Orief, is there a way to fix it or patch the spot that drips oil?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||11-16-2001||11:25 PM||||My '85 2.5 had a leak from the timing cover (crank pulley) seal.  It dripped a spot about the size of a dinner plate whenever i parked, and it slimed the cradle, control arm bushings, and motor mount.<P>It's fairly easy to change by removing the right rear wheel.  The pulley hub may also be scored; you may need to replace or sleeve it, although I smoothed mine with sandpaper.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||11-17-2001||12:56 PM||||Thank Pbody, Since I can't exactly pinpoint, I'm just goin to change it out anyways.  It probably can't hurt being 18 years old anyways.  And, besides, if its not leaking, I dont' want to get the other leak fix then have this go in about a week or 2.||||reg||1
